 "nodes": [
  {
   "id": "noteIntro",
   "type": "NoteNode",
   "x": -40,
   "y": -200,
   "init": {
    "label": "A tiny transformer",
    "color": "violet",
    "collapsed": false,
    "width": 820,
    "height": 560,
    "body": "# A tiny transformer\n\nA language model reads a prompt and guesses the next word. This is that calculation at toy size, every step on its own card: one layer, one attention head, four numbers per word and a five-word vocabulary. Read it left to right; each stage ends in a picture of what it made.\n\n1. **Read the prompt.** Each word of *the cat sat on* is looked up in the vocabulary: *the* is word 5, *cat* word 1.\n2. **Embed.** Each word's number picks its row out of the embedding table, so every word becomes four numbers. Words used the same way get similar rows: in the heatmap, *cat* and *mat* light up the same column. Each position then adds its own pattern of sine and cosine waves, so the model can tell the first word from the fourth.\n3. **Attend.** Each word builds a query, what it is looking for, and a key, what it offers. Every query is multiplied by every key and divided by 2, the square root of the four numbers per word, which keeps the scores in a workable range. The mask hides the words that come later, and the Softmax turns each row of scores into weights that add up to 1 (Edit Contents shows how). Each word then takes the weighted mix of the words it can see and adds it to itself. In the attention heatmap, *sat* and *on* both look almost entirely at *cat*.\n4. **Think.** A small network adjusts each word on its own. Its one hidden unit switches on for a preposition, and pushes that word toward predicting *the*.\n5. **Predict.** Each word's numbers are scored against every word in the vocabulary, and a Softmax turns the scores into probabilities. Every position guesses the word after it: after *the*, *cat* or *mat*; after *cat*, *sat*; after *sat*, *on*; after *on*, *the*.\n\nEvery weight here was set by hand so that each step can be read; training is how a real model finds its own, millions of them. Change things and watch: shorten the prompt to *the cat sat* and *on* becomes the favorite, or set the 3 in W_K's second row to 0 and attention stops singling out *cat*.",
    "fieldTypes": {}
   }
  },
